 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "WEST: Carlisle, Tullie House and the cathedral. EAST: Lanercost, Birdoswald and the Wall, with all of Northumberland beyond it. Hadrian does not like to be hurried, he is two thousand years old."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 30,
   "text": "A National Trail opened in 2003. It runs 84 miles, 135 kilometres, from Wallsend on the Tyne to Bowness-on-Solway, following the line of the Wall as closely as the farmland and the law allow. Walkers go either way, though the prevailing wind is behind you if you start in the west.",
   "plaque": "w55e_wall_path",
   "title": "The Hadrian's Wall Path"
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "Founded in 1169 by Augustinian canons, and built largely from stone robbed out of the nearby Roman Wall. Edward I of England stayed here through the winter of 1306 to 1307, already ill, on his way to fight Robert the Bruce. He died the following July, less than twenty miles away at Burgh by Sands, still on the road north.",
   "plaque": "w55e_lanercost",
   "title": "Lanercost Priory"
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "RIVER IRTHING. Crossed by the Romans on a stone bridge here: the abutment is still in the bank. The river takes no notice of either."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "The Roman fort of Banna, on a spur above the Irthing gorge. West of the river the Wall was first built of turf rather than stone, and only rebuilt in stone later, which is why you can still see how the two kinds of Wall differ. There is a fine stretch of standing Wall here. After the Romans left, a family with a timber hall lived inside the fort walls for generations.",
   "plaque": "w55e_birdoswald",
   "title": "Birdoswald Roman Fort"
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE VALLUM. A deep flat-bottomed ditch with mounds on both sides, running south of the Wall. Archaeologists still argue about what it was for. The best guess is that it marked the military zone and kept the locals out."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "MILECASTLE. A small gate fort, one for every Roman mile of the Wall, with two watchtowers between it and the next. Big enough for a few soldiers and a lot of sandwiches."
  },
  {
   "x": 68,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "EAST: the Wall proper, Housesteads and the Sycamore Gap, then Newcastle. WEST: Lanercost and Carlisle. Mind the wind, and mind the sheep."
  }
 ],
